How did we find this flag?

The OWASP article explains how to test a website for information leakage:
https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/v41/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/03-Review_Webserver_Metafiles_for_Information_Leakage
One of the things it talks about is the robots.txt file.

What is the robots.txt file?
It is a plain text file that defines rules for web spiders, robots, or crawlers (more details on the names: https://www.robotstxt.org/faq/othernames.html).
These are programs used by search engines (for example, Google) to automatically find and index web content.

The file contains rules that say which paths on the site are allowed to be crawled and indexed, and which are not.
This rules format is called the Robots Exclusion Protocol, a standard for giving instructions to robots on how to crawl websites.

How does a robot use robots.txt?
Before crawling a website, a search engine robot usually checks if there is a robots.txt file, for example:
http://www.your_site.com/robots.txt

The file typically contains directives like:
User-agent: which robot the rules apply to
Disallow: a path that the robot should not visit (not crawl)
Allow: a path that is explicitly allowed (often used together with Disallow)

Not all robots follow these instructions: malicious bots can ignore robots.txt entirely.
You cannot really protect your site from such bots using robots.txt, because: 
the protocol is only a recommendation, not an access control mechanism + you would need to block them in other ways (for example, by IP filtering, authentication, or other server‑side security settings).

Also, the robots.txt file is publicly accessible, and it does not require authentication.
Anyone can open it and see which areas you do not want search engines to index. Because of this, robots.txt should not be used as a way to protect or hide sensitive content. It does not replace proper access control, authentication, or authorization.

Alternatives to robots.txt
If you want to prevent search engines from indexing a specific page, you can use an HTML meta tag in that page:
xml
<meta name="robots" content="noindex">

If you want to tell robots not to follow the links on that page, you can use:
xml
<meta name="robots" content="nofollow">
(Just like robots.txt, these tags only work with well‑behaved search engine robots and are not a real security control.)

How is robots.txt created?
To let robots find it, the robots.txt file is placed in the root of the website, next to index.html or the main entry point of the site, so that it is available at:
https://example.com/robots.txt.

You can find more detailed information about robots and robots.txt here:
https://www.robotstxt.org/faq.html
